00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.589 align:start position:0%
 
do you need to know how to code to work  in tech no hey everyone it's lucy here  hope you're all doing well in this video  i will be talking about roles in tech  that don't require writing any lines of  code  if you're new to this channel i'm a  solutions architect graduate based in  australia working at aws  and because i studied information  systems in university which is part of  business school  i don't have very strong coding skills  so during my time in university i was  still interested in tech companies but  was more looking for roles within  product management  business development sales marketing and  so on  and so in this video i'm going to be  going through five of these roles the  pros and cons of each  what to expect and what skills important  forward height for candidates in these  positions  let's get right into it  the product manager role is great for  someone who likes to have a big picture  vision and love seeing how everything  fits in together  you're ultimately responsible for the  product strategy and take ownership of  the product roadmap  you work a lot with engineers designers  and other people across the company to  turn product ideas into  reality you also work a lot with the  users of the product  figure out what their pain points are  and come up with improvements to delight  the customer  let's say you work for a tech company  and one of their products is an iphone  app  you'll need to make sure the app meets  customer needs while also aligning with  business objectives  when hiring product managers companies  look for people who have an  entrepreneurial spirit  great teamwork skills and of course an  interest in the product  and because product managers work with  engineers and other technical people all  the time  it does help to have a technical  background but it's not necessary since  you won't be the one actually writing up  the code or designing the layout of the  different screens  moving on to the pros and cons the  product manager role is very dynamic  with a broad range of responsibilities  so every day will look quite different  which is great for someone who likes  variety  the role involves a lot of strategic  planning and stakeholder management  which is a great way to develop your  soft skills if you ever want to go for a  leadership position later down the line  however as a product manager you won't  be involved in actually developing the  solution you come up with which can be a  downside if you're someone who wants to  be a bit more hands-on with building or  implementing the product  the second role is the lead development  representative or business development  representative  a lead development representative or ldr  for short is someone who generates leads  and new business opportunities for the  company  it's a role within the sales team and  they spend a lot of time reaching out to  potential customers through phone calls  emails social media and in-person events  it can be quite a rewarding role for  people who enjoy a fast-paced  environment and also being comfortable  with interacting with strangers and  handling rejection  let's say you work for a tech company  that focuses on creating products that  small businesses can use to track their  expenses  as an ldr your role will be to seek out  new users for the product by contacting  a wide range of small businesses and  explaining to them how the products can  help them  when hiring for ldrs companies look for  people who are very ambitious and have  strong communication skills  the sales team is usually the first  point of contact for new and potential  customers  so being able to represent the company  and make a good first impression is very  important  moving on to the pros and cons a lot of  roles within sales are commission based  meaning that there's high earning  potential which means that you're  incentivized to work hard and smart to  deliver results  another benefit of working in sales  especially if you enjoy being around  people  is that every day you'll be interacting  with new faces and building new  relationships  however since many sales roles are very  focused on hitting metrics and targets  there's added pressure and stress if  your performance is consistently lower  than the goal  so whilst this pressure might help  encourage some to keep pushing forward  others might feel disheartened and  stressed by it  [Music]  the third rule is the content designer  content writer or content strategist  different companies like to use  different role titles but essentially a  content designer's main role is to  create  update and review the content of a  user's journey  it's great for someone who's passionate  about storytelling and being concise and  meaningful with their words  if you click on the website of a large  tech company any text you see on the  different pages was probably written by  a content designer and updated regularly  when needed  their role is to make sure that every  word and sentence is being used  accurately and appropriately to convey a  company's mission  and the product's vision when hiring for  content designers companies look for  people who have strong writing and  content strategy skills and have a way  of creatively engaging users through  text  having a portfolio of previous articles  blogs and technical writing would also  be a great way to showcase the skills  that you can bring  next we have the ux or product designer  a ux designer's role is to improve the  interaction between users and products  they take a human first approach when it  comes to designing a product and focus  on the full experience of a user  when companies hire software developers  to create a product they'll be able to  create something that functions properly  and takes all the boxes  but it might not be the most visually  appealing or user friendly  and this is where ux designers come in i  used to think a ux designer was someone  who spends the time creating  graphics and illustrations but in  reality a ux designer develops user  flows  creates prototypes and wireframes and  conducts user testing  when hiring for ux designers companies  look for people who are not only good at  coding but also have great communication  skills and the ability to empathize with  the customer  experience with prototyping and  wireframing tools is preferred so if  this role sounds interesting to you  have a look into tools like sketch and  figma moving on to the pros and cons  one of the most rewarding parts of being  a ux designer is seeing your design  being used in a product that can  potentially reach millions of customers  for those who love being creative and  hands-on the role can be a great way for  you to continuously improve your design  skills  because you receive so much feedback  from users and people in your team  on the other hand though since ux  designers receive a lot of feedback from  everyone  it may take a while for design decisions  to be made because of technology and  budget constraints your designs might  not get implemented which  sometimes give you a bit disheartening  last but not least we have the customer  success manager  the customer success manager or csm for  short works with customers to make sure  they receive you the right support to  achieve their goals  they're responsible for the customer's  success and so they're the ones  proactively contacting clients to check  up on how they're doing  if you're somewhat good at relationship  management and you love helping  customers solve their problems then the  csm role is a great role to consider  tech companies hire csms to make sure  there's a strong customer satisfaction  and retention once customers have  started using the product  very similar to the lead development  representative role the csm spends a lot  of time talking with new people  so if you enjoy forming new  relationships you'll find the world  pretty interesting  unlike the ldi role these relationships  tend to be more ongoing as customers  continue to face new challenges and  priorities  however because the csm acts as an  enabler between the company the product  and its customers  they don't directly work on improving  existing products or services  and this can be seen as a downside for  some people  thanks so much for watching i hope you  got something out of it please give it a  like if you did  and subscribe for more tech career and  student advice  bye for now

